WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Speaker 1: Let's go to Dean from&nbsp;
Dallas, Texas. Dean, welcome to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.469
the program. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 3: Hey, David, can you hear me?&nbsp;

00:00:06.469 --> 00:00:09.540
Speaker 1: I can.
Speaker 3: I was wondering, are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.400
familiar with South Korean politics at all?
Speaker 1: Only in passing.&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.060
Speaker 3: So South Korea has kind of had&nbsp;
a historical problem with their presidents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:27.390
They've sent Lee Myung bak for embezzlement and&nbsp;
bribery. He's gone to jail for a long time. Park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:33.360
in here is a female president, and she's been to&nbsp;
jail for her abuse of power, bribery, coercion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.360
leaking government secrets. Even the former&nbsp;
President Moon, he's under investigation. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.420
could go to jail. My question is to why a&nbsp;
democratic country like South Korea has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.640
issues prosecuting and jailing former presidents&nbsp;
and politicians. Politicians, but the U.S. can't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.420
Do you think culture and tradition trump law?
Speaker 1: I do. I do. And you know, if you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.120
to my birth country of Argentina, you'll see&nbsp;
there is also a long tradition of corruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.900
and arguable criminality from presidents and&nbsp;
leaders. And the last one, the previous one seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.440
almost always to be under investigation. And the&nbsp;
dynamics within countries, I think sometimes maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:24.060
the right term is that there are these over time&nbsp;
implicitly. It's not like people sit down and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.580
here we jail former leaders, and then in another&nbsp;
country, people sit down and say, here we do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:36.420
jail former leaders. I believe that it's small&nbsp;
decisions over time that are a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.340
how the judicial system and court&nbsp;
system, the judiciary is set up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:50.220
how judges are chosen, cultural attitudes towards&nbsp;
misdeeds by elected officials. All these little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:56.580
things together lead to some countries where it's&nbsp;
completely typical that you jail former leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.940
and others where you don't. It also exists in&nbsp;
the U.S. at the state by state level, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:05.340
look at New Jersey's history of prosecuting&nbsp;
former governors, which I mean, you could say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.020
well, they've had a lot more criminality among&nbsp;
former Jersey governors compared to another state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.580
But there also seems to be something there about&nbsp;
expectations. So I think it's all of these things.&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.580
Speaker 3: Okay. So you think like&nbsp;
the fear of public retribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.960
or violence impacts our justice system?
Speaker 1: That I don't know specifically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.760
It's possible. That's possible. But we'd really&nbsp;
have to ask more to understand people's attitudes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.780
But I think there is a cultural component&nbsp;
and there's also a sort of cumulative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.829
component over time related to expectations.
Speaker 3: Okay. Well, thanks for taking my call.&nbsp;

00:02:39.829 --> 00:02:44.040
Speaker 1: All right. Dean from Dallas,&nbsp;
thank you very, very much for the call.

